implementationPlan.description:
The fix, in Invoke-MoeRpc (scripts/moe-agent.ps1), with the Edit tool. The file must keep its UTF-8 BOM and LF endings, and every added character must be ASCII. Replace exactly these two lines (HEAD 9578c609 :1904-1905; match on the text, because n1 moves them):
                $text = $d.result.content[0].text
                try { return ($text | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction Stop) } catch { return $text }
with this block, text for text: it is FIX_NEW in /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-044c/apply.py, and mutants.sh anchors on it.
                $text = $d.result.content[0].text
                # Keep the reply's wire shape. A top-level JSON array, one-element and
                # empty ones included, comes back as ONE array, so an object consumer
                # can tell [{...}] from {...}: 5.1's ConvertFrom-Json emits an array
                # whole, pwsh 7 needs -NoEnumerate, and ',' keeps return from unrolling
                # it. Every tool this wrapper calls answers an object (task-044c7410).
                try {
                    $value = if ($PSVersionTable.PSVersion.Major -ge 7) { ConvertFrom-Json -InputObject $text -NoEnumerate -ErrorAction Stop } else { $text | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction Stop }
                } catch { return $text }
                if ($value -is [array]) { return ,$value }
                return $value

Why each part (measured with /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-044c/probe.ps1 on 5.1.26100 and 7.6.9):
- 5.1's ConvertFrom-Json writes a JSON array as ONE object. pwsh 7's enumerates it unless -NoEnumerate (7.0+); the -ge 7 test keeps unsupported pwsh 6 on today's behaviour.
- A function `return` of an array unrolls it, so arrays go back as `,$value`.
- Result: objects, scalars, null and non-JSON text come back exactly as before, and an array of 2+ items is the same Object[] as before. Only one-element and empty top-level arrays change: they used to come back as their item or as $null, and now come back as one array.
- The pass-through wrappers Invoke-MoeRpcUnserved and Invoke-MoeLandLeaseRpc (`return Invoke-MoeRpc ...`) and the gate's `6>$null` keep the shape (probe).

Do not touch:
- n1's gate: its PSCustomObject check already sends an array down today's path;
- Invoke-MoeRpcRaw (team setup): it returns the JSON-RPC envelope, which is always an object;
- the heartbeat job's Send-SidecarRpc;
- Get-MoeProp: it unrolls too, but it is a separate helper, out of scope, and n1 already avoids it;
- the sh twin: moe_rpc hands its consumers the raw JSON text, which python and node parse without unrolling.

Error handling: unchanged. A transport failure, an isError result and a JSON-RPC error still return $null with $script:MoeRpcFailure and $script:MoeRpcCodeName set exactly as before (those lines are untouched). Text that ConvertFrom-Json cannot parse still comes back as the raw text from the catch.

Adversarial self-review of this row's diff (adversarial-self-review skill), once, as the last step. Then call moe.complete_task with step 4's evidence and step 3's audit table in the summary.

(1) Scope. `git status --short` and `git diff --numstat` against your base show exactly two files:
- scripts/moe-agent.ps1, +10/-1 (the FIX_NEW block only);
- scripts/tests/postflight-vm-route.ps1, +1 (the row).
Nothing else: no edit to n1's gate, no other caller, no .moe/ path, no sh file. `head -c3 scripts/moe-agent.ps1 | od -An -tx1` prints `ef bb bf`, and the added lines are ASCII (`git diff | grep -P '[^\x00-\x7F]'` prints nothing).

(2) Mutation check, on a scratch COPY only. From your worktree run `bash /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-044c/mutants.sh` with run_in_background; it takes about 15 minutes. It reads your worktree, copies HEAD's scripts/, docs/roles and validators.ts plus your two changed files into /mnt/d/moe-scratch/044c-mut.*, and mutates only that copy. Each mutant must fail the copy's postflight-vm-route.ps1 at its row:
- M1 (today's return line) on 5.1: T-malformed-array;
- M3 (no comma) on 5.1: T-malformed-array;
- M2 (no pwsh 7 branch) on 7: T-malformed-array;
- M4 (every value wrapped in an array) on 5.1: C1, the first refusal row.
Expected: four `killed` lines and `mutants exit=0`. The architect's dry run killed 4 of 4 (/mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-044c/mutants.output.txt). A `NOT APPLIED` line means your text differs from FIX_NEW: match it. Never mutate the tracked file.

(3) Edge cases, confirmed by reading, not by new code:
- objects, scalars, null and raw text are unchanged (probe);
- an array of 2+ items is unchanged; one-element and empty arrays now come back as arrays;
- the pass-throughs keep the shape;
- pwsh 6 keeps today's behaviour;
- concurrency is unchanged: one proxy process per call, and no shared state is added.

(4) If any tracked byte changed in this step, rerun step 4.

